question:
An algebra course can be scheduled in three ways during the five-day school week:
\begin{itemize}
\item it meets every one of the five days from 10:00 to 11:00 am;
\item it meets from 10:00 to 11:15 am on four of the five days;
\item it meets from 10:00 to 11:40 am on three of the five days.
\end{itemize}
In how many different ways could the course be scheduled?

solution:
\textbf{At most $8$ cubes fit.} A cube placed in the box occupies $[a,a+4]\times[b,b+4]\times[c,c+4]$ with $0\le a,b,c\le6$. The open interval $(a,a+4)$ contains $3.5$ when $a<3.5$ and contains $6.5$ when $a>2.5$, so it always contains $3.5$ or $6.5$. Hence the interior of every cube contains one of the $8$ points whose coordinates are all $3.5$ or $6.5$. Non-overlapping cubes have disjoint interiors, so each of these points lies inside at most one cube, and there are at most $8$ cubes.

\textbf{Eight cubes fit}, in a $2\times2\times2$ block filling an $8\times8\times8$ corner of the box:

